Ö×œÕ"ÍAfter three months of every day usage I'm updating this deck's review (and it's "sequels"), with a negative review, because in hindsight, after three months of every day usage, it's just not good.

I still believe that the core idea of having a word in different contexts, conjugations and usages is good. I don't care if there are two cards with two sentences for one words, there is an extra review but it's easy, quick, and, most importantly, it's useful.

The issue with this deck stems from single word audio only cards. I find myself advantaged by audio, I can remember audio far better than I can remember text, especially Kanji.

However, having a card saying ã›ã‚“ as in ç·š and as in åƒ makes no sense. It's indistinguishable. Either you end up, for every single card, listing all the synonyms, or you cannot correctly guess which is which. You also cannot say in hindsight "I got it" because you don't know if you would have got it under the correct situation. Either remove these audio only no kanji cards, or have the audio have enough context to distinguish between "line" and "thousand".
Nobody will lend you a line, and it's weird to ask someone to write two red thousands in the middle a sheet of paper.

Same thing with æŒã¤ one of the first words in the first deck meaning "possess", also present in deck 3 as "maintain".

Another example is ã®ã‚‹ at first given as "become" such as "become a doctor" and then as "become ripe"... yet, the front is always just ã®ã‚‹... How are you supposed to guess like this? It shouldn't even be a guess, it should be one, single unequivocally correct answer.

This has made me waste a massive, and I mean a MASSIVE amount of time flagging cards wrong that I probably know, but technically didn't guess right over and over again, and the reason is, quite frankly, that the card itself is bad, especially for a language such as a Japanese where a lot of words are effectively the same sound-wise. (In some cases there is a pitch accent difference, but in some cases there is not.)

I also believe that an appropriate definition would be better than a "sort of random English translation", heck, a "broken" hard-coded English 1:1 translation equivalent would be better than the "creative" localization that this deck does. Some Japanese sentences which you are supposed to understand are translated to English set phrases, which make absolutely no sense, you are not learning a word, you are merely learning an unusable Anki card.
Consider that some cards are related to a note which is translated as a certain English word, to then find the sentence card of the very same note NOT HAVING that English word in the translation... sure the concept is there, but it sucks done like this.
A Tae Kim literal translation approach would have been far, far better.

Over three months, at the third deck, I can say that this deck is not good. And, unfortunately, you will find out later down the line when it's too late (or a massive set back anyway) to change deck.

Finally, some of the images just make no sense, like the one for ã—ã¦... it's a girl "supposedly" under a desk looking for her purse... excuse me what? What value is there in such an image?

I have done the math, I will need around another 6 months just to finish this core 2k deck at about 30 new cards a day, for what effectively is like 3 to 5 new words per session, with a lot of indistinguishable cards. There are, in all honesty, way too many card for what is a core 2k deck. I supposedly know 1931 card and 772 notes.... and I have just now finished deck 2 and started deck 3. Those 772 likely are not 772 words, though I might be wrong (and I hope I am). Point is there are too many cards for the actual words you learn.

Thank you to whoever did this for the effort, but please, find something else and don't bother with this one, because YOU ARE paying the cost of it later down the line.
